  "id": "gp.gerund-or-infinitive",
  "level": "B1",
  "topicId": "topic.verb-patterns",
  "title": "-ing or to after verbs",
  "summary": "Learn when to use a verb ending in -ing or 'to' + infinitive after another verb.",
  "explanation": "Some verbs are followed by another verb ending in *-ing*. For example, you *enjoy reading* or *finish working*.\n\nOther verbs are followed by *to* and the base form of the verb (the infinitive). For instance, you *decide to go* or *plan to visit*.\n\nIt's important to learn which verbs take -ing and which take 'to' + infinitive, as there isn't a simple rule. Common verbs followed by -ing include: enjoy, finish, mind, suggest, avoid, practice. Common verbs followed by 'to' + infinitive include: decide, plan, want, hope, need, agree, promise.",
  "patterns": [
   "verb + verb-ing",
   "verb + to + verb"
  ],
  "examples": [
